`context/instructions/ui-tokens.md` is the reference for the values that make a
look consistent: colour, spacing, type and radius. It is **conditional**: it
applies only when the project already has a token layer or design system. Read it
with `context/instructions/ui.md` (the doctrine),
`context/instructions/ui-motion.md` (motion's own value family) and
`context/instructions/ui-adapters.md` (how tokens map to a framework's theming).

**When this applies.** Tokens or a design system exist to extend; without one,
**stop and ask** — never create a design system, and never introduce one as a side
effect of a change. The project's own system is the source of truth, and local
rules belong in the AGENTS.md project block, not here.

## Tokens by intent, not by appearance

- Name a token for the role it plays, not the value it holds:
  `action.primary`, `surface.raised`, `text.muted` — never `blue-500` or
  `gray-3`.
- A system may have three tiers, used in one direction:
  **primitive** (raw values) → **semantic** (roles and intent) → **component**
  (component-specific). Components reference semantic or component tokens; they
  never reference primitives or raw values.
- The intent name is what makes a theme swap and a redesign possible without
  touching every component.

## Naming

Use one shape per project: `{category}.{property}.{variant}-{state}` — for
example `action.destructive-default`, `surface.raised-hover`. The name encodes
role, variant and state; the value never appears in the name.

## One source per project

- Keep a single token source. Do not let the same value be defined in two
  places; a token that drifts is worse than no token.
- If a value is needed that no token expresses, add the token at the right tier —
  do not inline the value.

## Theme swap at the semantic layer

- Themes (dark/light, a brand variant) swap **semantic** values. Primitives stay
  stable; components are unaware of the theme.
- Never branch component code on the theme; express the difference as a token
  value.
- Check both themes when a component uses a new token.

## Extraction threshold

- Extract a token only for a value that recurs **three or more times with the same
  intent**. Premature abstraction is worse than duplication.
- If no system exists, do not infer one from a few values: stop and ask (see the
  no-op rule).

## Surface relationships

- **Concentric radius.** Nested rounded surfaces share a centre: the outer radius
  equals the inner radius plus the padding between them (commonly card radius
  20px, padding 8px → inner 12px); equal radii with no such relationship read as
  pinched. Escape hatch: once the padding is large enough that the two surfaces
  read as separate, the rule no longer applies — the threshold is the project's
  own scale.
- **Shadow for elevation, border for structure.** A border that exists only to
  create depth is a shadow; a border that carries structure or state (a divider,
  an input outline, a focus ring) stays a border. Never a depth-only border.
- **Image outline is a neutral.** A hairline outline on an image is a neutral
  black or white, never a tinted palette colour (commonly 1px pure black at ~10%
  in light, pure white at ~10% in dark), inset so it adds no layout. The project's
  own neutral wins.

## Ramp and roles

- **A ramp is steps; a role is a decision.** Name each step once (50…950) and map
  every role to a step — surface, surface-raised, border, text, text-muted, accent,
  on-accent. A colour that appears in the UI with no role is either a missing role
  or a mistake; the role inventory is the completeness check, not the palette size.
- **Both themes are tested, never inferred.** A token that resolves in one scheme
  and not the other is a defect the dark/light swap exposes; look at both before
  claiming the pair works.
- **Compliance is shown as a pair.** When a change moves a value to a token, show
  before and after on the same surface: the intent is the same, the literal is gone.

## Boundary

- Choosing or adopting a design system is a library-first decision
  (`context/instructions/development.md`): propose a shortlist, get the user's
  choice, record it in the project block.
- How tokens become a framework's theming primitive is the adapter contract
  (`context/instructions/ui-adapters.md`).
- State enumeration and component structure live in
  `context/instructions/ui-components.md`.
